      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y-4y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y-4y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)\\text{\u00a0}i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y-4y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =(4y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+4x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)\\text{\u00a0}i+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ \u2207f(1,1) & =(4\\cdot (1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1))+4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1))\\text{\u00a0}i+(2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(1)-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(1))\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =8 \\sin{(1 )}\\text{\u00a0}i-2 \\cos{(1 )}\\text{\u00a0}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j.$",
