        ":question/problem": "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,3)$?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}))\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 2x^{1}y^{1}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot x^{2}y^{0}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 6x^{2}y^{1}z^{5}\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{6}{z}\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207f(1,1,3) & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1},\\,\\frac{6}{3}\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e82,\\,1,\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\u2207f(1,1,3)}{\u2016\u2207f(1,1,3)\u2016} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}\u27e82,\\,1,\\,2\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $\u27e8\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}\u27e9.$",
