      },
      "question": {
        ":question/problem": "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2,2,2)$?",
        ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}))\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e8\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 6x^{5}y^{4}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{3}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{4}z^{3}\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e8\\frac{6}{x},\\,\\frac{4}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207f(2,2,2) & =\u27e8\\frac{6}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2}\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e83,\\,2,\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{\u2207f(2,2,2)}{\u2016\u2207f(2,2,2)\u2016} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}\u27e83,\\,2,\\,2\u27e9 \\\\ & =\u27e8-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $\u27e8-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}\u27e9.$",
